  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Nine hikers cut their tent open from inside. February 1959, the northern Urals. They ran out into minus thirty wearing almost nothing.",
        "visual_query": "ural mountains snow ridge",
        "visual_fallback": "winter mountain"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Searchers found them scattered down the slope. Two lay by a dead fire under a cedar, their hands burned from climbing it.",
        "visual_query": "siberian snow forest trees",
        "visual_fallback": "snow covered pines"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The injuries made it worse. Crushed ribs. A fractured skull. And almost no marks on the skin above them.",
        "visual_query": "soviet 1959 expedition photographs",
        "visual_fallback": "vintage archive photos"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The file was sealed for decades, so the theories grew instead: secret weapons tests, infrasound, even a yeti.",
        "visual_query": "remote russian winter wilderness",
        "visual_fallback": "empty snow valley"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 2020 Russian prosecutors reopened it and named a slab avalanche. A year later physicists modelled the slope doing exactly that.",
        "visual_query": "avalanche slab snow slope",
        "visual_fallback": "sliding snow"
      }
    ],
